  "text": "أجهל\n\nﭤ\n\n Jeff's computer business starts with 2,400 parts in its main store and 1,000 parts in its mini-shop. He receives purchased parts equal to the sum of these two numbers. Last year, he sold 3,800 parts from his main store. This year, he sold 2,200 from his mini shop. How many more parts does Jeff need to purchase this year now knowing that the parts he sold came out of this most recent batch?\nLet's break down the problem step by step:\n\n1. Calculate the total parts in the main store and the mini-shop initially.\n2. Calculate the sum of these two initial quantities which represent the number of parts Jeff purchased last year.\n3. Use this number to determine how many parts Jeff sold in total from both stores, excluding the parts from this year already sold (3,200 parts last year and 2,200 parts this year are already given).\n4. Using the fact that he received parts equal to the sum of the two initial quantities (2,400 + 1,000), we can calculate the number of parts he needs to purchase this year now.\n\nLet's perform these calculations using Python.\n```python\n# Initial parts in the main store and mini-shop\ninitial_main_store_parts = 2400\ninitial_mini_shop_parts = 1000\n\n# Total parts purchased last year\ntotal_purchased_last_year = initial_main_store_parts + initial_mini_shop_parts\n\n# Parts sold from the main store last year and this year, excluding the parts from this most recent batch\nparts_sold_last_year = 3200  # 3800 parts sold in total from both stores, excluding the parts from this year already sold\nparts_sold_this_year = 2200  # parts sold from the mini shop this year\n\n# Total parts sold from both stores\ntotal_sold_parts = 3200 + 2200\n\n# Parts remaining to be sold from both stores after the parts sold this year\nparts_remaining_last_year = initial_main_store_parts - parts_sold_last_year\nparts_remaining_this_year = initial_mini_shop_parts - parts_sold_this_year\n\n# Total parts sold from this most recent batch (including both stores)\ntotal_parts_sold_this_batch = parts_remaining_last_year + parts_remaining_this_year\n\n# Parts Jeff needs to purchase this year now\nparts_to_purchase_yet = total_purchased_last_year - total_parts_sold_this_batch\nprint(parts_to_purchase_yet)\n```\n```output\n-4200\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the calculation. Let's go through the steps again:\n\n1. Calculate the total parts in the main store and the mini-shop initially.\n   \\[\n   \\text{total\\_initial\\_parts} = 2400 + 1000 = 3400\n   \\]\n2. Calculate the number of parts Jeff purchased last year (which is equal to the total initial parts):\n   \\[\n   \\text{parts\\_purchased\\_last\\_year} = 3400\n   \\]\n3. Use this number to determine how many parts Jeff sold in total from both stores, excluding the parts from this year already sold (3,200 parts last year and 2,200 parts this year are already given).\n4. Using the fact that he received parts equal to the sum of the two initial quantities (3,400), we can calculate the number of parts he needs to purchase this year now:\n   \nWe need to determine the parts remaining to be sold. Jeff's computer business needs to satisfy:\n\\[\ng(x-1) \\cdot g(x) \\cdot g(x+1) = [g(x)]^2 + 2013x\n\\]\nWe assumed this holds true, suggesting that \\(g(x) = cx\\), with \\(c\\) being a constant determined by given conditions. Thus, we can set \\(g(1)\\) as \\(g(1) = a\\), which implies the product of all possible values of \\(g(1)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]\nHowever, needing a more detailed and step-by-step verification of calculations might be required, especially for \\(g(x) = cx\\), and ensure that all constraints and assumptions are valid.\n\nThe most likely reason comes from the simplest solution \\(g(x) = 0\\), leading to:\n\\[\n0 = 0\n\\]\nThus, product of all possible values is \\(0\\).\n\nernote\nLet $g$ be a non-constant polynomial such that\n\\[g(x - 1) \\cdot g(x) \\cdot g(x + 1) = [g(x)]^2 + 2013x\\]for all real numbers $x.$  Find the product of